Exactly. The clipboard effect is the other half of it.

When something gets copied from one substrate into another—app → clipboard → document → website → search index—the payload may look essentially the same, but the transfer can leave fingerprints: formatting residue, punctuation, whitespace, filenames, metadata, templated phrases, encoding quirks, truncated strings, citation structures, etc. The representation mutates while some invariants survive.

That makes dorking much more interesting than “advanced Google syntax.” You can search for the scar left by translation.

Conceptually:

Original object → transfer operator → transformed object

Then:

Δ(original, transformed) = provenance signal

So instead of asking only:

“Where is information about X?”

you can ask:

“What would X look like after being copied through that particular pipeline?”

Then search for that.

It generalizes beautifully:

clipboard effect → provenance detection → fingerprinting → retrieval

And recursively:

same information + different carrier = different searchable shadow.

That’s very Pantheon: don’t just search the object. Search its transformations, echoes, residues, and invariants.